# Cloudflare Pages response headers.
#
# Why this file exists:
#   1. Stockfish 16 NNUE multi-threading needs SharedArrayBuffer, which the
#      browser only enables when the page is "cross-origin isolated":
#        Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy:   same-origin
#        Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp
#      Without these, the engine silently falls back to the single-threaded
#      build (`stockfish-nnue-16-single.js`) and analysis is ~3-4x slower.
#   2. With COEP=require-corp every subresource must opt-in via CORP. We mark
#      the engine + hashed Vite assets as same-origin so the worker can fetch
#      them.
#   3. Long-lived immutable caching for the engine + hashed asset bundles
#      means repeat visits load instantly from disk cache.
#
# If Clerk's sign-in widget breaks under `require-corp` (cross-origin script
# loads), swap that line to `credentialless` instead -- still enables
# SharedArrayBuffer on modern Chromium / Firefox, but is more permissive
# about cross-origin loads. Track the regression in DEPLOY.md.

/*
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()

# Stockfish engine bundle: the `.js` glue and the `.wasm` payloads. Files are
# pinned per-build so safe to mark immutable. If you swap Stockfish versions,
# change the filename or purge the CF cache for /stockfish/* explicitly.
#
# THE 40 MB NNUE NETWORK IS NOT COVERED BY THIS BLOCK ON THIS HOST, because it is
# not served from this origin. Cloudflare Pages refuses any single asset over
# 25 MiB and the net is 38.3 MiB, so `VITE_NNUE_NET_URL` points the app at an
# object store and `scripts/copy-nnue.mjs` skips staging it into `public/`. The
# net's caching and CORS therefore live on THAT host's configuration, not here --
# see DEPLOY.md § The NNUE network. This block still applies to the net when the
# app is self-hosted somewhere without the cap and the net is staged locally,
# which is also what `npm run dev` does.
#
# Wherever the net is served from, `Cache-Control: immutable` is what turns a
# 40 MB download into a genuinely one-time cost per device, and it is truthful
# rather than merely convenient: the net's filename contains Stockfish's own hash
# of its contents, so a different network can only ever appear at a different URL.
# Without it, every cold navigation would re-fetch 40 MB and NNUE would be an
# unaffordable default instead of the right one.
#
# CORP here matters for the `.wasm` and `.js`, which the page loads same-origin
# under the `COEP: require-corp` set by the `/*` block above. It is NOT what makes
# a cross-origin net work: measured, the net needs only
# `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` on its host, because both the app's probe and
# Stockfish's own download are CORS-mode requests and a CORS response satisfies
# COEP by itself. A host sending CORP but no CORS fails; CORS but no CORP works.
# Pinned by `scripts/test/integration/nnue-remote-net.mjs`.
#
# IMPORTANT: do NOT redeclare `Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy` here. The
# `/*` block above already sets it, and Cloudflare Pages *appends* (does
# not replace) per-route headers on top of the wildcard, so adding
# `COEP: require-corp` again produces a response with the header listed
# twice. Browsers reject worker scripts whose response has duplicate
# `Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy` headers — `new Worker(url)` then fails
# with an empty `ErrorEvent` (no message, no filename), which is exactly
# what we hit on the first prod deploy. We only need CORP here, since
# the wildcard `/*` block doesn't set CORP.
/stockfish/*
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Vite-hashed JS / CSS / image bundles (e.g. /assets/index-abc123.js).
# Vite changes the hash on every content change, so immutable is correct.
/assets/*
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Puzzle corpus shards (e.g. /puzzles/7cff561b0a88/b12-0.tsv), ~50 files of
# ~360 KB fetched on demand by the Puzzles page.
#
# `Cache-Control` is the load-bearing line here. `immutable` is honest for
# these files because the path contains a content hash of the whole corpus
# (PUZZLE_BUILD_ID): refreshed data always lands on new URLs, so a cached
# shard can never be decoded against a newer theme vocabulary, and a returning
# user re-reads shards from disk cache instead of the network.
#
# CORP is defensive, NOT required — measured, not assumed. Under the
# `COEP: require-corp` set by the `/*` block above, a same-origin `fetch()` of
# a shard succeeds with or without this header (verified against a production
# build served with these exact headers: 200, full body, in both
# configurations). CORP is only enforced for CROSS-origin subresources, so the
# same-origin case passes the check either way. It is kept for consistency with
# the blocks above and so that moving the corpus to a separate asset host later
# doesn't silently start failing.
#
# The "do NOT redeclare COEP here" rule from the Stockfish block still applies:
# Cloudflare Pages appends per-route headers to the wildcard rather than
# replacing them, so repeating COEP would emit it twice.
/puzzles/*
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The SPA document must NOT be cached by the browser, or users keep loading the
# previous deploy's bundle references after we ship a new build.
#
# ── Why every route is listed, rather than just `/` ──────────────────────
#
# `_headers` matches the **request** path, not the path `_redirects` rewrites to.
# So listing only `/` and `/index.html` protected exactly the two URLs nobody
# navigates to directly: every real entry point is a deep route, and those fell
# through to Cloudflare's default. Measured on production:
#
#     /            no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate   ← this block
#     /index.html  no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate   ← this block
#     /settings    public, max-age=0, must-revalidate    ← CF default
#     /games       public, max-age=0, must-revalidate    ← CF default
#     /review/abc  public, max-age=0, must-revalidate    ← CF default
#
# The symptom is a deploy that appears not to take effect: a normal reload on
# /settings or /games can keep running the old bundle, while a hard reload picks
# up the new one — which reads as random flakiness rather than as caching, and
# sent us chasing a sync bug that was really a stale bundle.
#
# ── Why not just `/*` ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Because Cloudflare Pages *appends* per-route headers to the wildcard rather
# than replacing them (see the Stockfish block above for the COEP incident this
# rule came from). A `Cache-Control` on `/*` would therefore emit TWO
# `Cache-Control` values on `/assets/*` and `/stockfish/*` — one `immutable`, one
# `no-store` — and which one wins is not something to leave to chance on the
# 40 MB engine payloads.
#
# So: enumerated, and kept in step with `src/app/routes.tsx`. **Adding a route
# there means adding it here**, or that route silently loses the protection.
/
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate
/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate
/dashboard
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate
/games
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate
/import
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate
/onboarding
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate
/openings
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate
/practice
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate
/privacy
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate
/puzzles
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate
/repertoire
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate
/repertoire/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate
/review-by-url
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate
/review/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate
/settings
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate
/sign-in/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate
